    "created_at": "2025-08-20T02:14:15Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-08-20T02:50:30Z",
    "body": "I do not understand how it moved from a discussion where there is almost no positive support to two implementation PRs. It seems like everyone is welcome to contribute allegedly as long as nobody suggests a change Google wants just shouldn't happen. (Even if there is some process normality to this, the optics of this process looks terrible from the outside.)\n\nCan the WHATWG core membership agree that there should be a mandatory public comment period and some need for positive support before attempting to delete web features? The web should be hard to break. Standards should be added carefully and slowly and removed almost never.\n\nWhat I really want to see is a clear commitment of a minimum bar of support to do things to the web platform. It should be possible for Google (or any other WHATWG core member) to suggest something and the community to be able to shoot that down. People get unruly because they have no agency. Decisions about the web are made for them, and they are silenced for speaking up. That's not a problem with the public, that's a problem with the WHATWG's process.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
